1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,989
Due diligence is the work of
checking claims and

2
00:00:02,989 --> 00:00:06,538
understanding terms before
making an investment decision.

3
00:00:06,538 --> 00:00:10,181
Start with the formal offering
documents, then compare the

4
00:00:10,181 --> 00:00:11,562
pitch with the filing.

5
00:00:11,562 --> 00:00:15,209
Can you explain what the
company sells, who its customers

6
00:00:15,209 --> 00:00:17,000
are, and why they might pay?

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,016
Check the team’s relevant
experience and identify what still

8
00:00:21,016 --> 00:00:22,288
needs to be proven.

9
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:26,834
Examine the financial statements,
cash needs, use of proceeds,

10
00:00:26,834 --> 00:00:29,400
ownership, and existing
securities.

11
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:33,025
Search SEC filings and read
amendments.

12
00:00:33,025 --> 00:00:36,910
Verify that any Reg CF
intermediary is registered, and

13
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:41,299
use FINRA BrokerCheck or the
SEC’s funding-portal information

14
00:00:41,299 --> 00:00:42,738
to check its status.

15
00:00:42,738 --> 00:00:46,805
Look for gaps, inconsistent
numbers, unsupported customer

16
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:50,588
or revenue claims, and unclear
conflicts of interest.

17
00:00:50,588 --> 00:00:55,200
Due diligence cannot predict the
future or uncover every problem.

18
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,704
It is a way to make your

19
00:00:56,704 --> 00:00:59,838
decision based on evidence
rather than excitement.

20
00:00:59,838 --> 00:01:04,019
If key facts are missing or you
cannot understand the security,

21
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:06,408
slowing down is a reasonable
choice.

